## 6. System Architecture

A representative system may include some or all of the following components.

### 6.1 Identity Service

The identity service authenticates users and maintains or retrieves identity records. Authentication may use:

- passwords;
- passkeys;
- public-key certificates;
- hardware authenticators;
- biometric authentication;
- federated identity;
- enterprise single sign-on;
- government or institutional credentials;
- decentralized identifiers; or
- multi-factor authentication.

### 6.2 Eligibility Classification Engine

The eligibility classification engine determines one or more user classifications. Inputs may include:

- employment status;
- professional license;
- citizenship or residency where legally permissible;
- educational enrollment or affiliation;
- training completion;
- security clearance or suitability status;
- project membership;
- contract status;
- sponsorship;
- age or legal-capacity status where relevant;
- research approval;
- regulatory qualification;
- account history; and
- manually reviewed documentation.

The classification may include:

- a classification label;
- classification source;
- effective date;
- expiration date;
- assurance level;
- confidence score;
- issuing authority;
- geographic or jurisdictional scope;
- allowed purposes; and
- revocation status.

### 6.3 Organization Verification Service

The organization verification service determines whether an associated organization exists, its type, and its status. It may evaluate:

- government or corporate registries;
- tax or charity records;
- accreditation records;
- professional or sectoral licenses;
- procurement or vendor systems;
- contractual databases;
- trusted identity providers;
- verified internet domains;
- digital certificates;
- payment or billing verification;
- institutional attestations;
- beneficial ownership information where lawful;
- prior review records; or
- combinations of independent evidence.

Organization classifications may be hierarchical or multi-valued. For example, an entity may be both a nonprofit organization and a research institution.

The verification service may distinguish among:

- asserted organization type;
- provisionally verified organization type;
- independently verified organization type;
- expired verification;
- disputed classification;
- unknown or unverified entity; and
- ineligible or restricted organization.

### 6.4 Affiliation or Relationship Verification Service

This service determines whether the user is affiliated with, sponsored by, employed by, contracted to, or otherwise authorized to represent the organization. Verification may be based on:

- an organization-managed identity;
- human-resources records;
- signed attestations;
- invitation or sponsorship workflows;
- contractual records;
- domain control;
- cryptographic credentials;
- periodic confirmation by an authorized representative; or
- multiple corroborating signals.

### 6.5 Policy Engine

The policy engine receives a structured authorization request containing, for example:

- user identifier or pseudonymous subject identifier;
- user eligibility classification;
- organization identifier and verified type;
- affiliation status;
- requested resource;
- requested operation;
- requested role;
- intended purpose;
- project identifier;
- authentication strength;
- device posture;
- network location;
- geographic region;
- time;
- behavioral or risk signals;
- resource sensitivity; and
- applicable legal, contractual, or tenant policy.

The policy engine produces a decision such as:

- permit;
- deny;
- permit with constraints;
- require approval;
- require additional evidence;
- require step-up authentication;
- provision isolated access;
- permit read-only access;
- permit access without export;
- defer pending review; or
- revoke existing access.

### 6.6 Role and Entitlement Manager

The role and entitlement manager maps a decision to one or more roles or permissions. It may:

- activate a predefined role;
- compose a role from permission fragments;
- generate a just-in-time role;
- issue a time-limited token;
- restrict a role to a project or resource subset;
- enforce separation of duties;
- require multiple approvers;
- cap resource consumption; or
- attach monitoring and logging obligations.

### 6.7 Resource Orchestrator and Enforcement Points

The resource orchestrator configures the secure environment according to the decision. Enforcement may occur at:

- an API gateway;
- identity-aware proxy;
- operating-system layer;
- hypervisor;
- container orchestrator;
- database;
- object store;
- network gateway;
- secure enclave;
- virtual desktop;
- job scheduler;
- model-serving endpoint;
- software-license manager; or
- physical or remote laboratory control system.

### 6.8 Continuous Evaluation Service

The continuous evaluation service receives changes or events, including:

- loss of employment or affiliation;
- expiration of a license or accreditation;
- change in organization type;
- suspension of an organization;
- policy updates;
- abnormal behavior;
- device compromise;
- resource reclassification;
- completion or termination of a project; or
- expiration of a verification record.

The service may revoke tokens, terminate sessions, reduce permissions, quarantine outputs, migrate workloads, or request re-verification.

### 6.9 Audit and Explanation Service

The system records:

- input attributes;
- data provenance;
- policy version;
- verification evidence or evidence references;
- decision result;
- decision rationale;
- issued entitlements;
- administrator overrides;
- access activity; and
- later revocation or modification events.

Sensitive evidence may be encrypted, tokenized, hashed, redacted, or retained separately from ordinary audit logs.

